# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-
"""Change-aware manuscript, part 3: Section 6 (Case study I: underground utility mapping)."""
HD("6. Case study I: underground utility mapping")
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ 6.1
HD("6.1 The survey evidence layer (E0)", 2)
P(f"The decision to release a segment rests first on a survey. Its reliability was analysed before any change was introduced, in the twelve scenarios of Section 5.4. In the matched model a sensor-quality-adjusted policy (P2: release if the hazard probability is at most 0.10, 0.02 in the marginal capability zone, never in the zone where detection capability is not established or when an on-site reference check fails) "
  f"releases {q('baseline','P2','coverage',0)}% of the corridor at a conditional risk of {q('baseline','P2')}%, a robust policy (P5: the supremum over parameter variants and structural alternatives of the filter) {q('baseline','P5','coverage',0)}% at {q('baseline','P5','cond_risk',2)}%, and standard Bayesian fusion with a 0.5 threshold {q('baseline','B1','coverage',0)}% at {q('baseline','B1')}%. "
  f"The conditional risk is a steep function of the released coverage (Fig. 2); at equal coverage the policies lie close together, and no policy is nowhere worse than another in more than {max(sum(ST['dominance'][n][p]['A_dominates_everywhere'] for n in SCN) for p in ST['dominance']['baseline'])} of the twelve scenarios (Supplementary Table S2).", align="justify")
FIGX(doc, os.path.join(FIG5, "fig23_reliability_coverage.png"), 6.4, "**Fig. 2.** Risk–coverage curves of the survey evidence layer: conditional false-clearance risk against released coverage in six scenarios (logarithmic axis; means over 10 independent networks of 700 km; bands are 95% t-intervals). The dashed curve is an oracle with the true sensor parameters, soil and health states.", alt="Risk-coverage curves of the survey layer")
P(f"**Overconfidence under mismatch.** Where the nominal model is wrong the picture changes. With a parallel utility and a branch (G4) P2 releases {q('G4_parallel_branch','P2','coverage',0)}% at a conditional risk of {q('G4_parallel_branch','P2',d=0)}% while the probabilities it attaches to those segments predict "
  f"{'%.1f' % (100 * SCN['G4_parallel_branch']['decision_gap']['P2_quality']['predicted'])}%; the robust policy holds {q('G4_parallel_branch','P5')}% at {q('G4_parallel_branch','P5','coverage',0)}% coverage, at the price of {'%.0f' % (100 * (qv('G4_parallel_branch','P2','coverage') - qv('G4_parallel_branch','P5','coverage')))} points of coverage, "
  f"and calibrated abstention on a plain score is in the same region ({q('G4_parallel_branch','RC1')}% at {q('G4_parallel_branch','RC1','coverage',0)}%). An unflagged GPR failure leaves every policy at about {q('B_gpr_failed_unflagged','P2',d=0)}% conditional risk; only an oracle that knows the sensor health ({q('B_gpr_failed_unflagged','B5')}%) is safe. "
  f"Combining the failures is neither additive nor independent: a failed unflagged GPR with the wrong geometry gives {q('B_x_G4','P2',d=0)}%, below the failure alone, whereas multiplying the marginal risks as independent would give {'%.0f' % (100 * (1 - (1 - qv('B_gpr_failed_unflagged','P2')) * (1 - qv('G4_parallel_branch','P2'))))}%.", align="justify")
l0, l3, l4 = GM[0]["models"], GM[3]["models"], GM[4]["models"]
P(f"**When added model complexity reduces reliability.** In the matched model a lateral-tracking filter with three parallel passes, which follows a utility that moves sideways, has a false clearance of {pc(l0['proposed_3pass']['metrics']['false_clearance'][0])}% against {pc(l0['continuity_1d']['metrics']['false_clearance'][0])}% for a one-dimensional continuity filter. "
  f"Under structural mismatch involving a parallel utility, or a parallel utility and a branch, it has {pc(l3['proposed_3pass']['metrics']['false_clearance'][0])}% and {pc(l4['proposed_3pass']['metrics']['false_clearance'][0])}%, against {pc(l3['continuity_1d']['metrics']['false_clearance'][0])}% and {pc(l4['continuity_1d']['metrics']['false_clearance'][0])}% for the simpler filter (Fig. 3). "
  "The tracker attributes the returns of the parallel line to the best-fitting offset, takes the line for the target and clears the footprint on which the real utility lies. A model that is more capable when its assumptions hold can therefore be the less reliable one when they do not: it becomes overconfident when its assumed spatial structure does not represent the network. "
  "This is the same hazard that the change-aware framework addresses for the process of change, and it motivates the gate.", align="justify")
FIGX(doc, os.path.join(K.FIG4, "fig20_geometry_mismatch.png"), 6.3, "**Fig. 3.** Deterioration as the true geometry departs from the filters’ model (L0 matched to L4: bends with a parallel utility and a branch): (a) false clearance among released segments, (b) false-alarm rate, (c) calibration error of the probability.", alt="Structural mismatch")
D2s, D5s = DEC["by_policy"]["P2_quality"], DEC["by_policy"]["P5_robust_theta_struct"]
P(f"**Aleatory and epistemic contributions.** Over a parameter envelope of {DEC['n_theta_draws']} draws crossed with five structural models of the geometry, the epistemic share of the variance of the unsafe-release probability of a simulated network of {DEC['network_size_corridors']} corridors is {'%.0f' % (100 * min(ES['P2_quality']))} to {'%.0f' % (100 * max(ES['P2_quality']))}% for P2 and "
  f"{'%.0f' % (100 * min(ES['P5_robust_theta_struct']))} to {'%.0f' % (100 * max(ES['P5_robust_theta_struct']))}% for P5 (Fig. 4): more simulated networks would not reduce it, only knowledge of the sensor parameters would. For P2 the mean unsafe-release probability is {'%.1f' % (100 * D2s[SM[0]]['mean_P_F'])}% for the matched geometry and {'%.1f' % (100 * D2s[SM[4]]['mean_P_F'])}% for a parallel utility and a branch; "
  f"for P5 it is {'%.2f' % (100 * D5s[SM[0]]['mean_P_F'])}% and {'%.2f' % (100 * D5s[SM[4]]['mean_P_F'])}%. A global sensitivity analysis shows that the dominant failure driver depends on the policy: the share of corridors with a parallel utility and a branch for P2, undetected sensor failure for P5. "
  "The structural models are scenarios and are not averaged; no single risk figure across them is claimed.", align="justify")
FIGX(doc, os.path.join(FIG5, "fig26_aleatory_epistemic.png"), 6.3, "**Fig. 4.** Aleatory and epistemic contributions to the unsafe-release probability of the survey evidence layer: (a) share of the variance that is epistemic (dark; 95% bootstrap intervals) and aleatory (light) per structural model; (b) distribution over the parameter envelope (boxes: quartiles; whiskers: 5th to 95th percentile; triangles: maxima).", alt="Aleatory and epistemic contributions")
SUPPF = [("fig22_failure_tree.png", FIG5, "**Fig. S1.** (a) Conceptual decomposition of unsafe release into detection, model, decision and verification failure; (b) attribution of the simulated unsafe releases of the sensor-quality-adjusted policy to mechanisms."),
         ("fig24_policy_tradeoff.png", FIG5, "**Fig. S2.** Safety-coverage trade-offs of the release policies at their own operating points in six scenarios (95% cluster-bootstrap intervals)."),
         ("fig25_fault_combinations.png", FIG5, "**Fig. S3.** Fault combinations in the survey layer: conditional risk and coverage of four policies; the diamond is the risk obtained by combining the marginal risks as independent."),
         ("fig27_ablation.png", FIG5, "**Fig. S4.** Ablation of the survey layer at equal released coverage."),
         ("fig28_global_sensitivity.png", FIG5, "**Fig. S5.** Global sensitivity (Sobol indices) of the unsafe-release probability and the conditional risk of three policies of the survey layer."),
         ("fig29_verification_prioritisation.png", FIG5, "**Fig. S6.** Share of the corridor cleared by trial pits against the pit budget for three allocation orders."),
         ("fig30_decision_calibration.png", FIG5, "**Fig. S7.** Risk predicted by the filter among released segments against the observed risk, four policies, twelve scenarios."),
         ("fig13_lateral_robustness.png", K.FIG4, "**Fig. S8.** Robustness of the lateral-tracking filters to lateral displacement of the utility."),
         ("fig14_sensor_health.png", K.FIG4, "**Fig. S9.** Classification under sensor degradation and failure in the lateral-tracking experiments."),
         ("fig17_out_of_distribution.png", K.FIG4, "**Fig. S10.** Seen and unseen soils with and without the on-site reference check."),
         ("fig08_equal_coverage.png", K.FIG4, "**Fig. S11.** Clearance at equal coverage: modeled intersections per km against released coverage."),
         ("fig15_capability_zones.png", K.FIG4, "**Fig. S12.** Capability zones by soil, utility and depth (closed form)."),
         ("fig21_parameter_envelope.png", K.FIG4, "**Fig. S13.** Parameter-uncertainty envelope of the lateral-tracking experiments."),
         ("fig02_detectability_depth.png", K.FIG, "**Fig. S14.** Fused signal detection probability against depth for each soil and utility type (closed form).")]
for _f, _d, _c in SUPPF:
    FIGX(doc, os.path.join(_d, _f), 6.3, _c, alt=_f)
P("What the survey layer does not contain is time. Everything above treats the true state as fixed while it is observed. The following sections remove that simplification.", align="justify")

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ 6.2
HD("6.2 Dynamic changes and observation staleness (E1, E2)", 2)
r1, a1, b1 = ratio_at(UT, "S0", "D2", 0.30, "dynamic_vs_static")
r2, a2, b2 = H1_UT
P(f"Between surveys the corridor changes: {DU['lam_A'] + DU['lam_U']:.4f} modification events per segment and epoch, {int(100 * DU['lam_A'] / (DU['lam_A'] + DU['lam_U']))}% of them authorized, and an event creates a hazard with probability {DU['theta_A']} (authorized) or {DU['theta_U']} (unauthorized). "
  f"A static twin of the surveys (S0) releases {E1(UT,'S0','coverage',0)}% of the segments at a conditional risk of {E1(UT,'S0','cond_risk',2)}% and misses {m6(DY[UT]['methods']['S0']['delay']['share_missed'],0)}% of hazard onsets; "
  f"the dynamic estimator without work records (D0) releases {E1(UT,'D0','coverage',0)}% at {E1(UT,'D0','cond_risk',2)}%, and with the work records as context (D2) {E1(UT,'D2','coverage',0)}% at {E1(UT,'D2','cond_risk',2)}% with a mean detection delay of {m6(DY[UT]['methods']['D2']['delay']['mean_delay'],1,1)} epochs against {m6(DY[UT]['methods']['S0']['delay']['mean_delay'],1,1)}. "
  f"At matched decision coverage (30%) the static twin has {r1:.1f} times the conditional risk of D2, and static-state fusion with continuous sensors {r2:.1f} times ({a2*100:.2f}% against {b2*100:.2f}%), which supports H1 for this domain (Fig. 5, Fig. 9). "
  f"The ad hoc decay (S0d) does not solve the problem: relaxing the twin toward the prior removes the coverage ({E1(UT,'S0d','coverage',0)}% of the segments released) and its risk at that coverage ({E1(UT,'S0d','cond_risk',2)}%) is higher than D2’s ({m6(cvx(R6['dynamic_vs_static'][UT]['methods']['D2'],'curve',0.08),2)}%).", align="justify")
FIGX(doc, os.path.join(FIG6, "fig32_staleness.png"), 6.5, "**Fig. 5.** Observation staleness. Conditional risk among released decisions against the number of epochs since the last survey for (a) utilities and (b) the scaffold (means over test sites with 95% intervals; points with less than 1% of decisions released are omitted); the dashed curve is the risk predicted by the dynamic estimator itself. (c) Utilities: ratio of the unsafe-release probability of the static twin to that of the dynamic estimator over event-rate multipliers and survey intervals (estimators recalibrated for each rate).", alt="Staleness")
P(f"The risk of the static twin grows with the age of the last survey, from {AGE(UT,'S0','cond_risk',0)}% just after a survey to {AGE(UT,'S0','cond_risk',11)}% eleven epochs later; that of D2 from {AGE(UT,'D2','cond_risk',0)}% to {AGE(UT,'D2','cond_risk',11)}%, and the risk the dynamic estimator predicts for its own released segments ({AGE(UT,'D2','predicted',11)}% at eleven epochs) follows the observed one. "
  "The estimator therefore also answers how fast confidence should fall: at the rate its change model implies, and not at a fixed factor. "
  f"The benefit depends on the regime (Fig. 5c): the ratio of the unsafe-release probabilities of the static and the dynamic estimator ranges from {min(REGIME.values()):.1f} to {max(REGIME.values()):.1f} over event-rate multipliers from 0.25 to 4 and survey intervals from 6 to 48 epochs, "
  f"and is {REGIME['1.0|12']:.1f} at the nominal rate and interval. It is largest where changes are frequent and surveys are rare, which is where a static map is least trustworthy.", align="justify")

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ 6.3
HD("6.3 Work-order reconciliation (E3)", 2)
P(f"With a point sensor on every segment, so that the sensor evidence on each change is the same, the three treatments of the work orders differ only in how they use them. Among changes made under a valid work order, the estimator that suppresses permit-matched changes (D1) flags {PE(UT,'D1','change_valid_permit','flagged')}% of the segments, "
  f"against {PE(UT,'D0','change_valid_permit','flagged')}% for the estimator without work records and {PE(UT,'D2','change_valid_permit','flagged')}% for the one that uses them as context (Fig. 6), "
  f"and leaves {PE(UT,'D1','change_valid_permit','hazard_missed',1)}% of those segments hazardous and unflagged, against {PE(UT,'D0','change_valid_permit','hazard_missed',1)}% and {PE(UT,'D2','change_valid_permit','hazard_missed',1)}%. "
  "Authorization is not proof of safety: an authorized change creates a hazard in half the cases, and suppressing it removes the warning exactly where the hazard is. "
  f"Overall, D1 misses {m6(PERM[UT]['D1']['delay']['share_missed'],1)}% of the hazard onsets, D0 {m6(PERM[UT]['D0']['delay']['share_missed'],1)}% and D2 {m6(PERM[UT]['D2']['delay']['share_missed'],1)}%. "
  f"The context estimator is not free: it escalates {m6(PERM[UT]['D2']['overall']['false_escalation_rate'],1)}% of the safe segment-epochs, against {m6(PERM[UT]['D0']['overall']['false_escalation_rate'],1)}% without work records. "
  f"For attribution, D2 is right in {PEA(UT,'D2','accuracy_when_decided',1)}% of the reported changes it classifies as authorized or unauthorized but leaves {PEA(UT,'D2','uncertain')}% of them uncertain, which is the correct response when the records are missing or wrong; the estimators without records cannot classify any change.", align="justify")
FIGX(doc, os.path.join(FIG6, "fig33_permits.png"), 6.5, "**Fig. 6.** Work-record reconciliation with a point sensor on every element: (a, d) share of elements flagged (verify or escalate) two epochs after the event, (b, e) share hazardous and not flagged, for the four cases of Table 2, and (c, f) attribution at reported changes (dark: correct when decided; light: left uncertain), for utilities (top) and the scaffold (bottom).", alt="Permit reconciliation")
P(f"The first row of the cases is a reminder that the survey, not the records, causes most false flags in this domain: {PE(UT,'D2','no_change_no_permit','flagged')}% of the elements without a change are flagged, because the survey false-alarm probability and the blind cells leave their probability above the release threshold. "
  "H2 is therefore supported in the sense that matters for safety: using the records as context did not increase the rate of undetected hazardous states relative to ignoring them (it lowered it slightly), and suppressing matched changes increased it several-fold.", align="justify")

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ 6.4
HD("6.4 Hybrid sensing (E4)", 2)
P(f"Continuous point monitors on 5%, 10% and 20% of the segments were compared with periodic surveys alone and with both, at a cost counted in node equivalents (a node costs {DU['node_cost']:.0f}; a survey {DU['survey_cost']:.0f}). With only the baseline survey and monitors on 10% of the segments (cost {R6['hybrid'][UT]['point_only_10']['cost']:.0f}) the corridor can be released over only {HY(UT,'point_only_10','coverage',d=0)}% at a conditional risk of {HY(UT,'point_only_10','cond_risk',d=2)}%; "
  f"surveys every 12 epochs alone (cost {R6['hybrid'][UT]['survey_only_12']['cost']:.0f}) release {HY(UT,'survey_only_12','coverage',d=0)}% at {HY(UT,'survey_only_12','cond_risk',d=2)}% with a mean delay of {HYD(UT,'survey_only_12','mean_delay',d=1,s=1.0)} epochs; "
  f"adding monitors on 10% or 20% of the segments (costs {R6['hybrid'][UT]['hybrid_10_12']['cost']:.0f} and {R6['hybrid'][UT]['hybrid_20_12']['cost']:.0f}) gives {HY(UT,'hybrid_10_12','cond_risk',d=2)}% and {HY(UT,'hybrid_20_12','cond_risk',d=2)}% with delays of {HYD(UT,'hybrid_10_12','mean_delay',d=1,s=1.0)} and {HYD(UT,'hybrid_20_12','mean_delay',d=1,s=1.0)} epochs; "
  f"halving the survey interval to 6 epochs (cost {R6['hybrid'][UT]['survey_only_6']['cost']:.0f}) gives {HY(UT,'survey_only_6','cond_risk',d=2)}% and {HYD(UT,'survey_only_6','mean_delay',d=1,s=1.0)} epochs (Fig. 7). "
  "In this domain the cadence of the survey is the dominant lever, and point monitors on a small share of the segments add little, because most hazard onsets occur at segments that no monitor observes. The value of the hybrid architecture is its dependence on where the monitors are, which is the question of the next experiment.", align="justify")
FIGX(doc, os.path.join(FIG6, "fig34_hybrid.png"), 6.4, "**Fig. 7.** Hybrid sensing: cost (in sensor-node equivalents) against (a, b) the share of hazard onsets not flagged and (c, d) the conditional risk among released decisions, for configurations of point sensors with a baseline survey only (pN: N% of the elements), periodic surveys only (sI: interval of I epochs) and both (hN/I), for utilities (left) and the scaffold (right); decision-level coverage in the Supplementary Material.", alt="Hybrid sensing")

# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ 6.5
HD("6.5 Sensor placement (E5)", 2)
P(f"At a budget of 10% of the segments the unsafe-release probability of D2 is {PL(UT,'0.1','random','unsafe_prob')}% with random placement, {PL(UT,'0.1','uniform','unsafe_prob')}% with uniform spacing, {PL(UT,'0.1','uncertainty','unsafe_prob')}% with uncertainty-based placement and {PL(UT,'0.1','risk_optimised','unsafe_prob')}% with risk-optimized placement; "
  f"at 20% they are {PL(UT,'0.2','random','unsafe_prob')}%, {PL(UT,'0.2','uniform','unsafe_prob')}%, {PL(UT,'0.2','uncertainty','unsafe_prob')}% and {PL(UT,'0.2','risk_optimised','unsafe_prob')}% (intervals in the Supplementary Material; Fig. 8). "
  "Risk-optimized placement is the lowest in this domain at 10% and 20%, by about 0.03 and 0.05 percentage points; the half-widths of the intervals are about 0.02, so the difference is within the intervals at 10% and about two half-widths at 20%. Uncertainty-based placement is the worst, probably because the segments least observable by the survey are also those in blind cells, where a point monitor cannot create the missing baseline. "
  "The result is a small reduction of risk at the same sensor budget, not an increase in detection accuracy, and it should not be extrapolated to other budgets or domains.", align="justify")
FIGX(doc, os.path.join(FIG6, "fig35_placement.png"), 6.2, "**Fig. 8.** Sensor placement: unsafe-release probability of the dynamic estimator at three budgets for four allocation strategies (95% intervals over test sites), for utilities (a) and the scaffold (b).", alt="Sensor placement")
